7":"breaking","H2470":"weak","H4347":"wound","H1777":"to judge","H1779":"judgment","H4205":"wound","H7499":"remedy","H8585":"conduit","H157":"lover","H7911":"to forget","H1875":"to seek","H341":"enemy","H5221":"to smite","H4148":"discipline","H394":"cruel","H7230":"abundance","H5771":"crime","H6105":"be vast","H2403":"sin","H4100":"what?","H2199":"to cry out","H4341":"pain","H3651":"right","H398":"to eat","H6862":"narrow","H3212":"to walk","H7601":"to spoil","H4933":"plunder","H962":"to plunder","H957":"plunder","H5927":"rise","H724":"health","H7495":"to heal","H5080":"to banish","H7121":"call to","H6726":"Zion","H168":"tent","H4908":"tabernacle","H7355":"to have compassion","H1129":"to build","H5892":"excitement","H8510":"mound","H759":"palace","H3427":"to dwell","H3318":"come","H8426":"thanksgiving","H7832":"to laugh","H7235":"to multiply","H4591":"to diminish","H3513":"honour","H6819":"be little","H1121":"child","H6924":"East","H5712":"congregation","H3559":"prepare","H6485":"list","H3905":"to oppress","H117":"great","H4910":"to rule","H7130":"among","H7126":"come","H5066":"approach","H4310":"who?","H2088":"this","H6148":"to pledge","H3820":"heart","H595":"I","H5591":"tempest","H2534":"rage","H1641":"to drag/chew/saw","H7218":"head","H7563":"wicked","H2342":"tremble","H2740":"burning anger","H639":"anger","H5704":"till","H4209":"plot","H319":"end","H995":"to understand"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",9,null,"challoner","David.... That is, Christ of the house of David."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Book. This was spoken in the reign of Sedecias, for the people's conviction. The prophet had received orders to write in the 4th year of Joakim. C. xxxvi. 1. (Calmet)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Come. Some in S. Jerom explain this and the following chapter of the end of the world, when all shall confess Christ. Others refer them to the preaching of the gospel alone. (Estius; Tirinus) — But the return from captivity is specified, as prefiguring that event. S. Thomas, &c. (Calmet) — It is probable that many of the ten tribes returned to Samaria. C. xxxi. Ezec. xxxiii. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","To Juda. All the race of Abraham are concerned. The kingdom was no longer divided."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","We. Jeremias is ordered to express the alarms of the captives, at the news of the destruction of Jerusalem, or rather of the Chaldee empire, by Cyrus. They were naturally afraid that they would also suffer."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Bear. Literally \"beget,\" generat. But it has here the former signification, (Haydock) pariat. (Vatable) — Yellow. The Babylonians are in great anxiety. (Calmet)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Great, and terrible for this city, the outer walls of which shall be demolished, (Beros. C.) and all its glory perish. (Haydock) — Of it. Cyrus liberated the Jews. 1 Esd. i."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Strangers. Idols. The people were not so prone to worship them. Yet the Jews were almost constantly subject to foreigners (Calmet) despectissima pars servientium, Macedonibus invalidis...sibi ipsis reges imposuere. Tacit. Hist. 5. — Christ granted a more perfect liberty to the faithful. Jo. viii. 33. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"theodoret","David. That is, Christ, of the house of David. Ch. Ezec. xxxvii. 24. Osee iii. 5. — Grotius and some modern Jews, in opposition to their ancestors, (Chaldean Kimchi, &c.) and to all Christians, would understand Zorobabel, though he was never possessed of the title or authority of king. (Calmet) — The prophecy may allude to him, but it is fulfilled only in Christ. (Theodoret)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Nations, which are now no more. Grabe supplies v. 10. 11. 15. and 22. (Haydock) — Judgment, like a father, (Calmet) though the chastisement may seem cruel. v. 14. (Haydock) — Hebrew \"with justice, but I will not deny thee for ever.\" Chaldean \"utterly.\" (Calmet) — Only the Church is preserved continually. All other kingdoms change. (Worthington)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Up. There is none to judge thy cause, or to be thy physician. (Calmet)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Lovers. Nations which had seduced thee to worship their idols. (Haydock) — Enemy. This judgment (v. 11.) was requisite. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Prey. The Romans utterly overturned the Macedonian empire, (Haydock) as the former had done the Persian, and they the Chaldee monarchy, which has risen on the ruins of the Assyrian empire. But the Jews rise as it were from their ashes. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Close. Septuagint \"remove the healing plaster from thy painful wound.\" (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Temple. After 70 years, it was rebuilt. The Church was founded on a rock. (Worthington)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Play, or laugh. (Calmet) — Protestants \"make merry.\" C. xxxi. 4. — Lessened. The Jews were as numerous in our Saviour's time H. as ever they had been. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Leader. Zorobabel, the figure (Theodoret) of Christ, who is here meant. (Calmet) — He springs from Jacob. (Worthington) — The sceptre was not taken away till his coming. Gen. xlix. (Haydock) — Who. Cyrus alludes to this passage in his decree. 1 Esd. i. (Calmet) — The prediction is fully verified in Christians. (St. Jerome) — Christ is near to God, being one. Jo. xiv. (Worthington)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Wicked, at Jerusalem, or rather the Chaldees; and the Jews, who crucified their Messias. S. Jer. &c. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"theodoret":["Theodoret of Cyrus",0,1,457,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/14570a.htm"]},"fa":[[8,9,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 7",0,339,"… Compare with this the oracle in the Second Psalm concerning the Coming of Christ and the calling of the Gentiles, which says: \"Let us break their bonds asunder, and cast off their yoke from us. To which, I think, this we are considering is akin when it says: \"In that day, saith the Lord, I will break the yoke from off their necks, and shatter their bonds, and they shall not serve other gods, but shall serve the Lord their God.\""],[9,null,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 7",0,339,"… Therefore the prophecy in the present passage is that God will raise up \"a righteous rising\" to David, in the sense of a sun of righteousness. And he calls the same Being an understanding king, and one who does judgment and justice on the earth. He gives him too the same name as David, who died very long before. For you must note carefully how at the beginning he says, \"And I will raise up to David a righteous rising,\" and adds at the end, \"And I will raise up David to be his king. Whose, but David's?—for it was to him that he said He would raise up a righteous rising. …"]],"fau":[["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339]],"ftr":[["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"]]}